        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 735,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 721,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Applies the modified Bessel function of the second kind of order 0, K0,\nmultiplied by e to the x, to every element of a tensor.\n\nK0 describes how something spreading out from a long thin source falls\noff with distance - heat from a wire, or a field that is screened as it\nspreads - and this scaled form keeps it from underflowing far from the\nsource.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nDefined for positive x. Infinite at 0, falling steeply at first and then\nvery slowly, approaching the square root of (pi / 2x): 2.68 at 0.1, 0.39 at\n10. K0 itself dies away like e to the minus x; multiplying by e to the x\nremoves that so the numbers stay ordinary.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.scaled_modified_bessel_k0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\n0 gives infinity, negative inputs give NaN (not a number). Keep start\nabove 0.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (0.1 to 10 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is e to the x times K0(x) of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from 0.1 to 10,\ndrawn from 0 (bottom) to 3 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch (keep start above 0).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.i0e is the matching scaled version of I0, the growing\npartner of K0.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 705,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Applies the modified Bessel function of the second kind of order 0, K0,\nmultiplied by e to the x, to every element of a tensor.\n\nK0 describes how something spreading out from a long thin source falls\noff with distance - heat from a wire, or a field that is screened as it\nspreads - and this scaled form keeps it from underflowing far from the\nsource.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nDefined for positive x. Infinite at 0, falling steeply at first and then\nvery slowly, approaching the square root of (pi / 2x): 2.68 at 0.1, 0.39 at\n10. K0 itself dies away like e to the minus x; multiplying by e to the x\nremoves that so the numbers stay ordinary.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.scaled_modified_bessel_k0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\n0 gives infinity, negative inputs give NaN (not a number). Keep start\nabove 0.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (0.1 to 10 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is e to the x times K0(x) of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from 0.1 to 10,\ndrawn from 0 (bottom) to 3 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch (keep start above 0).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.i0e is the matching scaled version of I0, the growing\npartner of K0."
        },
